[{:db/id 17592186412202 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,4\\le z\\le 8-x,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-\\frac{x}{2}\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281288 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le y\\le 2z\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281287 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le 2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281300 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a7/a7261baec0c907779f5b1f5ff669ca826d4b93c5dd1b9a389e0989fe34756a6e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65c61ad7dc2964cd0de9a6bbdf259fcf3388e25da4eaabf155d58ff0c05c8d55.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-2y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-2y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-x-2y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4-x-2y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4-x)y-{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x-1\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3-x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(6-2)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412209 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c4/c4cf12ec9b53282620c1730f824c96d98e0b9dbabee0478bf258cf6c2741da81.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b497e6ef73b5b16e11baa08819e9628d3b5e1f2ee4d05fbafec48f73e087cbf1.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+y+z}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=0}^{x=1+y+z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+y+z)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+z\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(1+y)z+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}+y\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412194 :question/worked-solution "![](images/7c/7c1738e97bc16a32f86d6901136afc4d9e288035d170fcd640db340ad36d19c5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nTo find the top boundary curve of $D$ in the form $z=z(y),$ we substitute $x=1$ into the equation $x=2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z:$\n\n$$\n1=2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\,⟹\\,z=2-\\frac{2}{3}y\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{2}{3}y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea8b8397163309a11aa478fcc21ab53ee293c19f0d77a93b5c3d3472bb0eba70.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{2}{3}y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y/3-z/2}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2-y/3-z/2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}(2-\\frac{y}{3}-\\frac{z}{2})-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}2-\\frac{y}{3}-\\frac{z}{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(2-\\frac{y}{3})z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=2-2y/3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}((2-\\frac{y}{3})(2-\\frac{2y}{3})-\\frac{1}{4}{(2-\\frac{2y}{3})}^{2})-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2-\\frac{2y}{3})((2-\\frac{y}{3})-\\frac{1}{4}(2-\\frac{2y}{3}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2-\\frac{2y}{3})(\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{6})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3-\\frac{4y}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[3y-\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{27}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =(3(3)-\\frac{2(3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{3}^{3}}{27})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412207 :question/worked-solution "![](images/59/599b5392add89a6a22eb6c2232fedbdd26704ebf849bb05d7de52a6d81dcfb6a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e7c6fad45d9d85bad5ca16a2000e4d44a1739ab70797e41ae94945a4ffb02ef.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x/2-z}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=3-x/2-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3-\\frac{x}{2}-z)-0\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(3-\\frac{x}{2})z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((3-\\frac{x}{2})(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[4x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(8-2)-0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281292 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})[y]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =2(4(2)-\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}))-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412193 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,-{z}^{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[x]_{x=-{z}^{2}}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-(-{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[z\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=-1}^{z=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-(-1))\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\sqrt{y}+3\\,dy \\\\  & ={[2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}+3y]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}+3(1)-0 \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281293 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,1-y\\le x\\le y+1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{y+1}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=1-y}^{x=y+1}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y+1-(1-y)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129703 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412199 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le x+y+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x+y+2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=x+y+2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+y+2\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x+2+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x+\\frac{5}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x]}_{x=-1}^{x=0} \\\\  & =0-(\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412206 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9-4{y}^{2} \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 9-4{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $9{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{9-4{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-4{y}^{2})-(5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}}}\\,9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}})}^{2}+(9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =(9-9{y}^{2})+(9-9{y}^{2})+(9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =27-27{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412196 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}^{4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-(2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-1\\le x\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(2-2{x}^{2})+(2-2{x}^{2})+(2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235411 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}^{3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})-(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})+(1-2{x}^{2})+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4-8{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235409 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =1-2{z}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type III region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1-2{z}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the elipse $4{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{4{x}^{2}}^{1-2{z}^{2}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2{z}^{2})-(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{(1-4{x}^{2})/2}}^{\\sqrt{(1-4{x}^{2})/2}}\\,1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+(1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}+(1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-8{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235407 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is a circle.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the circle ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{y}^{2})-(3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}}/2}^{\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}}/2}\\,1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}})}^{2}+(1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-4{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}(1-4{y}^{2})+(1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-6{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-68138858-7af6-59b5-82a5-7a64f2b7d918" :activity/id #uuid "68138858-7af6-59b5-82a5-7a64f2b7d918" :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048694 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-a457e0db-1973-5b6b-8d71-9a6a062a4f73" "step-eb57df81-a928-539a-b8d1-a80b4295d9a1" "step-8f914621-4d05-5aa5-9db2-878a4bb76393" "step-007eb044-32d9-58ff-9c52-c7f147890537" "step-87361f89-76de-51cb-bf0f-80295869b0f0"] :activity/first-step "step-a457e0db-1973-5b6b-8d71-9a6a062a4f73" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a457e0db-1973-5b6b-8d71-9a6a062a4f73" :step/id #uuid "a457e0db-1973-5b6b-8d71-9a6a062a4f73" :step/content 17592186152741 :step/next "step-eb57df81-a928-539a-b8d1-a80b4295d9a1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-eb57df81-a928-539a-b8d1-a80b4295d9a1" :step/id #uuid "eb57df81-a928-539a-b8d1-a80b4295d9a1" :step/content 17592186455508 :step/next "step-8f914621-4d05-5aa5-9db2-878a4bb76393" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8f914621-4d05-5aa5-9db2-878a4bb76393" :step/id #uuid "8f914621-4d05-5aa5-9db2-878a4bb76393" :step/content 17592186364399 :step/next "step-007eb044-32d9-58ff-9c52-c7f147890537" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-007eb044-32d9-58ff-9c52-c7f147890537" :step/id #uuid "007eb044-32d9-58ff-9c52-c7f147890537" :step/content 17592186364406 :step/next "step-87361f89-76de-51cb-bf0f-80295869b0f0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-87361f89-76de-51cb-bf0f-80295869b0f0" :step/id #uuid "87361f89-76de-51cb-bf0f-80295869b0f0" :step/content 17592186364413 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186175036 :answer-field/choices 17592186175039] [:db/retract 17592186135604 :answer-field/choices 17592186135605] [:db/retract 17592186135604 :answer-field/choices 17592186135606] [:db/retract 17592186135604 :answer-field/choices 17592186135607] [:db/retract 17592186135604 :answer-field/choices 17592186135608] [:db/retract 17592186135604 :answer-field/choices 17592186135609] [:db/retract 17592186104470 :answer-field/choices 17592186104471] [:db/retract 17592186104470 :answer-field/choices 17592186104472] [:db/retract 17592186104470 :answer-field/choices 17592186104473] [:db/retract 17592186104470 :answer-field/choices 17592186104474] [:db/retract 17592186104470 :answer-field/choices 17592186104475] {:db/id 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3ba51678-bfa5-5eea-a51d-29e6a8b3feb3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ba51678-bfa5-5eea-a51d-29e6a8b3feb3" :question/id #uuid "3ba51678-bfa5-5eea-a51d-29e6a8b3feb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74587" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a1f9f1877ae0ecbc37464c5b8b20821070e7927e59dea8d930ceb8f37313722.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a parallelogram since it has $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since the opposite sides of a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite. It has no pairs of opposite sides that are parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $P,$ $Q,$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shape $S$ is not parallelogram." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b42fb396-a4b8-5570-82e5-99d092314e89"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b42fb396-a4b8-5570-82e5-99d092314e89" :answer-field/id #uuid "b42fb396-a4b8-5570-82e5-99d092314e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b0c121de-66bb-58ae-a63e-bbf43d0fe360" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f1f6f0ac-04f0-51ca-89d5-280f541bf412" "answer-aa794fee-92cb-5205-bbb7-3265b7d85083" "answer-b5636201-42e6-5b02-b154-839b34a298f1" "answer-b0c121de-66bb-58ae-a63e-bbf43d0fe360" "answer-58145878-10ab-57b3-b664-f6d36bca4d69"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1f6f0ac-04f0-51ca-89d5-280f541bf412" :answer/id #uuid "f1f6f0ac-04f0-51ca-89d5-280f541bf412" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa794fee-92cb-5205-bbb7-3265b7d85083" :answer/id #uuid "aa794fee-92cb-5205-bbb7-3265b7d85083" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5636201-42e6-5b02-b154-839b34a298f1" :answer/id #uuid "b5636201-42e6-5b02-b154-839b34a298f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0c121de-66bb-58ae-a63e-bbf43d0fe360" :answer/id #uuid "b0c121de-66bb-58ae-a63e-bbf43d0fe360" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58145878-10ab-57b3-b664-f6d36bca4d69" :answer/id #uuid "58145878-10ab-57b3-b664-f6d36bca4d69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096678 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0f0c1499-18ff-5c0e-854a-a4a1f5ed7366"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f0c1499-18ff-5c0e-854a-a4a1f5ed7366" :question/id #uuid "0f0c1499-18ff-5c0e-854a-a4a1f5ed7366" :question/math-academy-id "q-68484" :question/problem "The city of Mexicali, in Mexico, is $6.6$ feet below sea level, while the city of Porto Alegre, in Brazil, is $55.8$ feet above sea level. What is the difference between the elevations of both cities?" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the difference between the elevations of both cities, we must calculate the difference between the city with the highest elevation and the city with the lowest elevation. That is, we must calculate $55.8-(-6.6).$ This gives\n\n$$\n55.8-(-6.6)=55.8+6.6=62.4\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-efa5e756-b09f-5eba-be5d-3837c1691e2d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-efa5e756-b09f-5eba-be5d-3837c1691e2d" :answer-field/id #uuid "efa5e756-b09f-5eba-be5d-3837c1691e2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b01773cb-7e4c-5838-b4ba-57ccad0bdecd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2ce53447-7833-5af3-8f32-25e4bb25afb1" "answer-94e4a985-0800-55dd-82be-d6e16717c046" "answer-984a41f7-7d0c-5d85-9a6b-bd545d610281" "answer-dc6f0ca0-3f27-5c0d-a4ac-1e91b6462a1c" "answer-b01773cb-7e4c-5838-b4ba-57ccad0bdecd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ce53447-7833-5af3-8f32-25e4bb25afb1" :answer/id #uuid "2ce53447-7833-5af3-8f32-25e4bb25afb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49.2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94e4a985-0800-55dd-82be-d6e16717c046" :answer/id #uuid "94e4a985-0800-55dd-82be-d6e16717c046" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "52.1\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-984a41f7-7d0c-5d85-9a6b-bd545d610281" :answer/id #uuid "984a41f7-7d0c-5d85-9a6b-bd545d610281" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "58.6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc6f0ca0-3f27-5c0d-a4ac-1e91b6462a1c" :answer/id #uuid "dc6f0ca0-3f27-5c0d-a4ac-1e91b6462a1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60.3\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b01773cb-7e4c-5838-b4ba-57ccad0bdecd" :answer/id #uuid "b01773cb-7e4c-5838-b4ba-57ccad0bdecd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "62.4\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176483 :question/problem "What is $\\sqrt{{(-49)}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that by definition, the square root is always zero or positive, never negative.\n\nSo, the square root comes out to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-49)^{2}}=\\sqrt{{49}^{2}}=49.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175036 :answer-field/correct 17592186175040 :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b216cfc-dcbe-5847-9bef-9747d27351d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b216cfc-dcbe-5847-9bef-9747d27351d3" :answer/id #uuid "7b216cfc-dcbe-5847-9bef-9747d27351d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 49" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064258 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-15a43dbb-9c91-5be9-a586-59bfd5235547"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-15a43dbb-9c91-5be9-a586-59bfd5235547" :question/id #uuid "15a43dbb-9c91-5be9-a586-59bfd5235547" :question/math-academy-id "q-103627" :question/problem "$\\frac{2x-3y+8z}{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-3y+8z}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{2x}{6}-\\frac{3y}{6}+\\frac{8z}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{6}\\cdot x-\\frac{3}{6}\\cdot y+\\frac{8}{6}\\cdot z & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot y+\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot z & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{y}{2}+\\frac{4z}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-beaa49a3-09ca-5bd9-8b13-bb052a6be6fb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-beaa49a3-09ca-5bd9-8b13-bb052a6be6fb" :answer-field/id #uuid "beaa49a3-09ca-5bd9-8b13-bb052a6be6fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-77da7769-a1d4-5c71-8686-24a20677c965" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8ae6efe2-2a69-5edd-a91a-e313b3c19515" "answer-b90bf318-c0cb-58e1-9f0f-37146557bef8" "answer-154b90d1-09df-5975-9106-7abfa155a7f3" "answer-77da7769-a1d4-5c71-8686-24a20677c965" "answer-8274a2c9-bf10-549e-8286-c1d9ea4ef1c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ae6efe2-2a69-5edd-a91a-e313b3c19515" :answer/id #uuid "8ae6efe2-2a69-5edd-a91a-e313b3c19515" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-3y+4z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b90bf318-c0cb-58e1-9f0f-37146557bef8" :answer/id #uuid "b90bf318-c0cb-58e1-9f0f-37146557bef8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-2y+\\frac{4z}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-154b90d1-09df-5975-9106-7abfa155a7f3" :answer/id #uuid "154b90d1-09df-5975-9106-7abfa155a7f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-2y+4z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77da7769-a1d4-5c71-8686-24a20677c965" :answer/id #uuid "77da7769-a1d4-5c71-8686-24a20677c965" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{y}{2}+\\frac{4z}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8274a2c9-bf10-549e-8286-c1d9ea4ef1c0" :answer/id #uuid "8274a2c9-bf10-549e-8286-c1d9ea4ef1c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-y-\\frac{4z}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198382 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f462c87b-3e02-54b6-b206-e6def06b7c2e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f462c87b-3e02-54b6-b206-e6def06b7c2e" :question/id #uuid "f462c87b-3e02-54b6-b206-e6def06b7c2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162057" :question/problem "$(-5)÷4$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "Here, we are dividing a negative number $(-5)$ by a positive one $(4).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-5)÷4=-\\,\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that\n\n$$\n5÷4=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n(-5)÷4=-1\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-30c3593c-4e14-5e97-8e53-48b988006b27"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-30c3593c-4e14-5e97-8e53-48b988006b27" :answer-field/id #uuid "30c3593c-4e14-5e97-8e53-48b988006b27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-057d7c6e-29e6-5051-a5e8-b0998b5d0542" :answer-field/choices ["answer-057d7c6e-29e6-5051-a5e8-b0998b5d0542" "answer-2e6041d2-fb6a-5f6a-8e9c-714854eda1d9" "answer-f19ae37e-0dc6-5e4e-b694-e737cfd667d3" "answer-2b653396-bc27-5145-b8ac-e1022471deab" "answer-76d979a8-8760-59e0-812f-a0b817c69f44"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-057d7c6e-29e6-5051-a5e8-b0998b5d0542" :answer/id #uuid "057d7c6e-29e6-5051-a5e8-b0998b5d0542" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e6041d2-fb6a-5f6a-8e9c-714854eda1d9" :answer/id #uuid "2e6041d2-fb6a-5f6a-8e9c-714854eda1d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\,\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f19ae37e-0dc6-5e4e-b694-e737cfd667d3" :answer/id #uuid "f19ae37e-0dc6-5e4e-b694-e737cfd667d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b653396-bc27-5145-b8ac-e1022471deab" :answer/id #uuid "2b653396-bc27-5145-b8ac-e1022471deab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76d979a8-8760-59e0-812f-a0b817c69f44" :answer/id #uuid "76d979a8-8760-59e0-812f-a0b817c69f44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054786 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dcca9682-5c6e-581c-bcc3-60c96a3dddcd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dcca9682-5c6e-581c-bcc3-60c96a3dddcd" :question/id #uuid "dcca9682-5c6e-581c-bcc3-60c96a3dddcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-141915" :question/problem "$(-5)(2x+3y)-5y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we distribute the $(-5)$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}(-5)(2x+3y)-5y & = \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 2x+(-5)\\cdot 3y-5y & = \\\\ (-10x)+(-15y)-5y & = \\\\ -10x-15y-5y\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-10x-15y-5y & = \\\\ -10x+(-15y-5y) & = \\\\ -10x-20y\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-add26c3a-8ea0-579d-b7c2-eb977845c92f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-add26c3a-8ea0-579d-b7c2-eb977845c92f" :answer-field/id #uuid "add26c3a-8ea0-579d-b7c2-eb977845c92f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9183e516-17fb-5257-9c76-19c9409e87ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d17a4b4a-c1a9-581c-8038-8ac220d502da" "answer-9183e516-17fb-5257-9c76-19c9409e87ff" "answer-5ae20b64-ab72-5509-bc96-2ab8d1fafe50" "answer-272cdf46-670b-5698-b4dd-e9cb21841355" "answer-2147df49-a826-5ebb-a3dc-ebe444239e0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d17a4b4a-c1a9-581c-8038-8ac220d502da" :answer/id #uuid "d17a4b4a-c1a9-581c-8038-8ac220d502da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10x+15y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9183e516-17fb-5257-9c76-19c9409e87ff" :answer/id #uuid "9183e516-17fb-5257-9c76-19c9409e87ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10x-20y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ae20b64-ab72-5509-bc96-2ab8d1fafe50" :answer/id #uuid "5ae20b64-ab72-5509-bc96-2ab8d1fafe50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15x-15y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-272cdf46-670b-5698-b4dd-e9cb21841355" :answer/id #uuid "272cdf46-670b-5698-b4dd-e9cb21841355" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10x-8y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2147df49-a826-5ebb-a3dc-ebe444239e0f" :answer/id #uuid "2147df49-a826-5ebb-a3dc-ebe444239e0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-20y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138776 :question/worked-solution "The red segment contains all values between  $-2$ and $1.$ In addition,\n\n- the open circle at $-2$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $-2,$ while\n\n- the open circle at $1$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $1.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents $-2<x<1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135604 :answer-field/correct "answer-4592c636-8f62-592d-9c3e-276e70810db5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4592c636-8f62-592d-9c3e-276e70810db5" "answer-9871d376-12ef-5e6e-af44-fe57744b0653" "answer-9be6b73e-0418-56db-90da-473d702f855a" "answer-ef9054fd-d157-5ca3-9428-0aa2e99411c5" "answer-14295dff-21db-5fe1-a96c-8930ed638809"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4592c636-8f62-592d-9c3e-276e70810db5" :answer/id #uuid "4592c636-8f62-592d-9c3e-276e70810db5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2<x<1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9871d376-12ef-5e6e-af44-fe57744b0653" :answer/id #uuid "9871d376-12ef-5e6e-af44-fe57744b0653" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2<x\\le 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9be6b73e-0418-56db-90da-473d702f855a" :answer/id #uuid "9be6b73e-0418-56db-90da-473d702f855a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\le x<1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef9054fd-d157-5ca3-9428-0aa2e99411c5" :answer/id #uuid "ef9054fd-d157-5ca3-9428-0aa2e99411c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14295dff-21db-5fe1-a96c-8930ed638809" :answer/id #uuid "14295dff-21db-5fe1-a96c-8930ed638809" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x<-2$ or $x\\ge 1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110675 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f8c7f0d6-8dc6-549e-8375-7c33cf7277e3" "question-73e5dd6e-2c47-5055-afc2-9cc17f2d620e" "question-f44be398-7e63-5657-97ac-ad7b78dba187"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f8c7f0d6-8dc6-549e-8375-7c33cf7277e3" :question/id #uuid "f8c7f0d6-8dc6-549e-8375-7c33cf7277e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-155053" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a31a793df705b6cbe8dddb648ca6a0d2ebb6f4eb724ff8b99682f48cafb072a0.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" :question/worked-solution "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/aa/aad13ab456bc61c90522725ddb60f481c6138a9e53a50136d691ddd344349cf1.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/14/143e831321a485a0e57b706c6df89273bd92b3134d691d1ef41a67b7f7076481.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/2c/2c361eea2a598de063dc62375716ca5bdb7b0a06d4c30cf9a5c420cd9cc48e4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7892cfad-b772-5810-a92d-5b07af0c75e0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7892cfad-b772-5810-a92d-5b07af0c75e0" :answer-field/id #uuid "7892cfad-b772-5810-a92d-5b07af0c75e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-56e1f971-d113-5022-8210-b1ab22042601" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0af0a69-97d7-566d-ae7c-64e83031da5e" "answer-ea8c32c2-3154-5e95-ba13-18e6ee28fe41" "answer-56e1f971-d113-5022-8210-b1ab22042601" "answer-20256647-65f2-5046-a78f-8335ba715a35" "answer-1643603d-c5ae-57d3-9136-823babe8d447"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0af0a69-97d7-566d-ae7c-64e83031da5e" :answer/id #uuid "a0af0a69-97d7-566d-ae7c-64e83031da5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea8c32c2-3154-5e95-ba13-18e6ee28fe41" :answer/id #uuid "ea8c32c2-3154-5e95-ba13-18e6ee28fe41" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56e1f971-d113-5022-8210-b1ab22042601" :answer/id #uuid "56e1f971-d113-5022-8210-b1ab22042601" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20256647-65f2-5046-a78f-8335ba715a35" :answer/id #uuid "20256647-65f2-5046-a78f-8335ba715a35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1643603d-c5ae-57d3-9136-823babe8d447" :answer/id #uuid "1643603d-c5ae-57d3-9136-823babe8d447" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073900 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-553e8be5-17eb-5fb4-be35-fa84cdea996b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-553e8be5-17eb-5fb4-be35-fa84cdea996b" :question/id #uuid "553e8be5-17eb-5fb4-be35-fa84cdea996b" :question/math-academy-id "q-60651" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9b42a179625b667cb54f1b597127809d851a1b7a830a2632b09402ecb790330.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(3,2)$  and $(-2,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $5$ units, so the distance equals $5.$\n\n![](images/49/498674b4a4667b57be1574a081bbb948350754ed8993c14428ad8f31ee17bd90.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/f3/f3811804824e802c4cb20af85bac7f21f6c4e0f60e0430607c876e1cf27b7cd3.png)\n\nThe point $(-2,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(3,2)$ is at a distance of $3$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $2+3=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b85440a4-40ff-52cb-af1f-cc98ab1fcd3d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b85440a4-40ff-52cb-af1f-cc98ab1fcd3d" :answer-field/id #uuid "b85440a4-40ff-52cb-af1f-cc98ab1fcd3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bffc5c5f-557f-50da-ae87-c04af13e2bd7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-07de2851-2484-55be-a23c-13409d875b06" "answer-f8f314d3-fb52-56ee-b641-75480fce47ce" "answer-fac4f137-1d76-5d07-8f85-2cdd7cfafb4d" "answer-c7bc695b-36dc-5765-94b9-c570c3a39720" "answer-bffc5c5f-557f-50da-ae87-c04af13e2bd7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07de2851-2484-55be-a23c-13409d875b06" :answer/id #uuid "07de2851-2484-55be-a23c-13409d875b06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8f314d3-fb52-56ee-b641-75480fce47ce" :answer/id #uuid "f8f314d3-fb52-56ee-b641-75480fce47ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fac4f137-1d76-5d07-8f85-2cdd7cfafb4d" :answer/id #uuid "fac4f137-1d76-5d07-8f85-2cdd7cfafb4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7bc695b-36dc-5765-94b9-c570c3a39720" :answer/id #uuid "c7bc695b-36dc-5765-94b9-c570c3a39720" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bffc5c5f-557f-50da-ae87-c04af13e2bd7" :answer/id #uuid "bffc5c5f-557f-50da-ae87-c04af13e2bd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aeb2c9f4-4f11-5f7d-ac23-e92ca6ecec4e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-aeb2c9f4-4f11-5f7d-ac23-e92ca6ecec4e" :question/id #uuid "aeb2c9f4-4f11-5f7d-ac23-e92ca6ecec4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93027" :question/problem "A water tank with a capacity of $5,000$ liters is filled with water to $42.5%$ of its capacity. How much water is in the tank?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the tank, which is $5,000$ liters. The percentage is $42.5%,$ and the part is the amount of water in the tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n42.5%=42.5÷100=0.425\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =42.5%\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =0.425\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =2,125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42.5%$ of $5,000$ is $2,125.$ Thus, there are $2,125$ liters of water in the tank.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n42.5%=\\frac{42.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =42.5%\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{42.5}{100}\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =42.5\\times \\frac{5,000}{100} \\\\  & =42.5\\times 50 \\\\  & =2,125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42.5%$ of $5,000$ is $2,125.$ Thus, there are $2,125$ liters of water in the tank." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3f118c52-0f0a-5860-a202-cec610c20ffc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3f118c52-0f0a-5860-a202-cec610c20ffc" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f118c52-0f0a-5860-a202-cec610c20ffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2283e2bc-ebee-5c4d-a9ca-30fbe755b358" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2283e2bc-ebee-5c4d-a9ca-30fbe755b358" "answer-3ed97be4-c5cb-5b26-8151-563b09ffe246" "answer-daa496f1-eb97-5c4e-bcee-4319394a7cda" "answer-4fb0f9be-d361-51b6-99a5-14990f65e490" "answer-4140294a-4de5-516e-a556-10df8fbe6d5d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2283e2bc-ebee-5c4d-a9ca-30fbe755b358" :answer/id #uuid "2283e2bc-ebee-5c4d-a9ca-30fbe755b358" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2,125$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ed97be4-c5cb-5b26-8151-563b09ffe246" :answer/id #uuid "3ed97be4-c5cb-5b26-8151-563b0