-33cbe3a6bb3b" :step/next "step-ddd3f114-58f8-562c-960c-b5c342a5bcb5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ddd3f114-58f8-562c-960c-b5c342a5bcb5" :step/id #uuid "ddd3f114-58f8-562c-960c-b5c342a5bcb5" :step/content "question-aab3a1fe-f42d-5861-9d8c-8da472abefec" :step/next "step-b3d2d012-a306-590e-b68d-a518598a0966" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b3d2d012-a306-590e-b68d-a518598a0966" :step/id #uuid "b3d2d012-a306-590e-b68d-a518598a0966" :step/content "question-02fcb6dd-d8d1-548e-be72-9006c65abdf0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064159 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-25541256-5bc3-5680-8cfb-415ab2841d24"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-25541256-5bc3-5680-8cfb-415ab2841d24" :question/id #uuid "25541256-5bc3-5680-8cfb-415ab2841d24" :question/math-academy-id "q-2343" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ on the interval $[0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(0)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}-2\\sqrt{0}}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =1 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c188be5e-98c5-59a5-b9d1-5475ca041c52"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c188be5e-98c5-59a5-b9d1-5475ca041c52" :answer-field/id #uuid "c188be5e-98c5-59a5-b9d1-5475ca041c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-56368eb0-62cd-5b47-9649-8a52b20f1665" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0e9674ae-a147-5879-9568-2a43d2058b4f" "answer-4010086b-848a-5ea5-82de-eb24b3bb903e" "answer-56368eb0-62cd-5b47-9649-8a52b20f1665" "answer-7c35d7e8-37ab-5494-bf3b-b68ee9403499" "answer-65d4cdaf-8675-5f83-94c6-0e9ced9ebd24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e9674ae-a147-5879-9568-2a43d2058b4f" :answer/id #uuid "0e9674ae-a147-5879-9568-2a43d2058b4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\,,\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4010086b-848a-5ea5-82de-eb24b3bb903e" :answer/id #uuid "4010086b-848a-5ea5-82de-eb24b3bb903e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\,,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56368eb0-62cd-5b47-9649-8a52b20f1665" :answer/id #uuid "56368eb0-62cd-5b47-9649-8a52b20f1665" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c35d7e8-37ab-5494-bf3b-b68ee9403499" :answer/id #uuid "7c35d7e8-37ab-5494-bf3b-b68ee9403499" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,,-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65d4cdaf-8675-5f83-94c6-0e9ced9ebd24" :answer/id #uuid "65d4cdaf-8675-5f83-94c6-0e9ced9ebd24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064161 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b1af75bd-8ab0-5a82-8a2c-c57f1e5066dc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1af75bd-8ab0-5a82-8a2c-c57f1e5066dc" :question/id #uuid "b1af75bd-8ab0-5a82-8a2c-c57f1e5066dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-48398" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,2]$ and differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $s(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ and differentiable on $(-1,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$   Hence, is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it  is not continuous on $[-1,2].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.5.$   Hence, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$  Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1a9534ff-8431-5338-90d3-6411bea7e74e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a9534ff-8431-5338-90d3-6411bea7e74e" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a9534ff-8431-5338-90d3-6411bea7e74e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ac0dd388-1826-523a-8f01-94e20b377afb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac0dd388-1826-523a-8f01-94e20b377afb" "answer-361edf4f-989a-5330-8717-5e168840ee5f" "answer-fc586a0d-ed5f-586b-95eb-1d8d3942c34d" "answer-1831d77f-6d94-535e-ba17-45caba096ee5" "answer-9a397c28-aebc-5165-84c8-77548e6c6984"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac0dd388-1826-523a-8f01-94e20b377afb" :answer/id #uuid "ac0dd388-1826-523a-8f01-94e20b377afb" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/49/498484746591fb92b30dc1dd6e85b5c09aa92beb87685942b2d750461cbe8d0d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-361edf4f-989a-5330-8717-5e168840ee5f" :answer/id #uuid "361edf4f-989a-5330-8717-5e168840ee5f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4b/4ba47b9edb9b81c308f10cd894f30647e0717d72c2e4b410fab64d8b6d363013.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc586a0d-ed5f-586b-95eb-1d8d3942c34d" :answer/id #uuid "fc586a0d-ed5f-586b-95eb-1d8d3942c34d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/71/717c5ea51719c9f25058408d34b40fc2d380047805f987d505ee950dfbe87c7b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1831d77f-6d94-535e-ba17-45caba096ee5" :answer/id #uuid "1831d77f-6d94-535e-ba17-45caba096ee5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b0/b00ecf32276a82367046a6cdc46a17ea16b7fd3a035db7fb89125129f4a98f36.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a397c28-aebc-5165-84c8-77548e6c6984" :answer/id #uuid "9a397c28-aebc-5165-84c8-77548e6c6984" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c6/c6dd83eb0ca7715be6eae23d99b994fb5c999c723174360ab76891b13524e320.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-225c5ace-861b-5b33-b01d-abc5c6cb132b" "question-1d671ef3-a015-5e44-a360-62d57b505322"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-225c5ace-861b-5b33-b01d-abc5c6cb132b" :question/id #uuid "225c5ace-861b-5b33-b01d-abc5c6cb132b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77862" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[2,4]?$\n\nI. $f(x)={e}^{x}+1$\n\nII. $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n\nIII. $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[2,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{x}+1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions I, II, and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[2,4].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9b8e4d58-166a-531b-89d6-01969b1f483c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9b8e4d58-166a-531b-89d6-01969b1f483c" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b8e4d58-166a-531b-89d6-01969b1f483c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fef64265-7919-570e-9e0f-096179d44242" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25b53ada-f0fa-5620-859d-46e155938572" "answer-a9ff7038-2739-5c1e-85fc-4d2fdc8e845d" "answer-fef64265-7919-570e-9e0f-096179d44242" "answer-890029d8-4943-5351-a156-ee82a4b37c23" "answer-4b606b0d-c85c-5360-bdd7-67fd8245daac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25b53ada-f0fa-5620-859d-46e155938572" :answer/id #uuid "25b53ada-f0fa-5620-859d-46e155938572" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9ff7038-2739-5c1e-85fc-4d2fdc8e845d" :answer/id #uuid "a9ff7038-2739-5c1e-85fc-4d2fdc8e845d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fef64265-7919-570e-9e0f-096179d44242" :answer/id #uuid "fef64265-7919-570e-9e0f-096179d44242" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-890029d8-4943-5351-a156-ee82a4b37c23" :answer/id #uuid "890029d8-4943-5351-a156-ee82a4b37c23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b606b0d-c85c-5360-bdd7-67fd8245daac" :answer/id #uuid "4b606b0d-c85c-5360-bdd7-67fd8245daac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1d671ef3-a015-5e44-a360-62d57b505322" :question/id #uuid "1d671ef3-a015-5e44-a360-62d57b505322" :question/math-academy-id "q-77866" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\nI. $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}-1$\n\nIII. $h(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}-1}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}$ is not defined at $x=\\pm 1,$ hence, it is not continuous at $x=\\pm 1,$ and so it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}-1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}-1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29ef2aff-afe9-507f-b7de-dc2ad513b4a3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29ef2aff-afe9-507f-b7de-dc2ad513b4a3" :answer-field/id #uuid "29ef2aff-afe9-507f-b7de-dc2ad513b4a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8a89ac6f-c58e-5f2e-b088-1f3e54ca549f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1d6750e-77d4-5a7f-9f58-2a3cb2171378" "answer-3c1ef230-0d99-5c1f-8d27-3c9c320c2d37" "answer-64f8f555-b29a-5712-b45f-4405639612a0" "answer-8a89ac6f-c58e-5f2e-b088-1f3e54ca549f" "answer-e0f14253-e313-5d66-a24f-f584115125d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1d6750e-77d4-5a7f-9f58-2a3cb2171378" :answer/id #uuid "e1d6750e-77d4-5a7f-9f58-2a3cb2171378" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c1ef230-0d99-5c1f-8d27-3c9c320c2d37" :answer/id #uuid "3c1ef230-0d99-5c1f-8d27-3c9c320c2d37" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64f8f555-b29a-5712-b45f-4405639612a0" :answer/id #uuid "64f8f555-b29a-5712-b45f-4405639612a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a89ac6f-c58e-5f2e-b088-1f3e54ca549f" :answer/id #uuid "8a89ac6f-c58e-5f2e-b088-1f3e54ca549f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0f14253-e313-5d66-a24f-f584115125d2" :answer/id #uuid "e0f14253-e313-5d66-a24f-f584115125d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064956 :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $s(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has corner points at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $h(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=\\pm 1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not continuous on $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=1.$ Hence, $t(x)$  is not continuous on $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063791 :answer-field/correct 17592186063793 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5a848c20-0415-5b02-b95f-ce0a23286fca" :activity/id #uuid "5a848c20-0415-5b02-b95f-ce0a23286fca" :activity/title "The Mean Value Theorem" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048434 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-0b76c144-7c47-52de-9ea9-c39e20d2a05c" "step-110a422c-b425-51b1-9039-ab22fcb85ae9" "step-4388043b-7356-590d-9a11-82470f55a57a" "step-ceb8fe3c-9091-5a0f-b315-d9d7d76bdc62" "step-87d4cbb3-7160-5663-9f62-7416ecf098d0"] :activity/first-step "step-0b76c144-7c47-52de-9ea9-c39e20d2a05c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-0b76c144-7c47-52de-9ea9-c39e20d2a05c" :step/id #uuid "0b76c144-7c47-52de-9ea9-c39e20d2a05c" :step/content "question-25541256-5bc3-5680-8cfb-415ab2841d24" :step/next "step-110a422c-b425-51b1-9039-ab22fcb85ae9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-110a422c-b425-51b1-9039-ab22fcb85ae9" :step/id #uuid "110a422c-b425-51b1-9039-ab22fcb85ae9" :step/content "question-b1af75bd-8ab0-5a82-8a2c-c57f1e5066dc" :step/next "step-4388043b-7356-590d-9a11-82470f55a57a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4388043b-7356-590d-9a11-82470f55a57a" :step/id #uuid "4388043b-7356-590d-9a11-82470f55a57a" :step/content "question-225c5ace-861b-5b33-b01d-abc5c6cb132b" :step/next "step-ceb8fe3c-9091-5a0f-b315-d9d7d76bdc62" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ceb8fe3c-9091-5a0f-b315-d9d7d76bdc62" :step/id #uuid "ceb8fe3c-9091-5a0f-b315-d9d7d76bdc62" :step/content "question-1d671ef3-a015-5e44-a360-62d57b505322" :step/next "step-87d4cbb3-7160-5663-9f62-7416ecf098d0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-87d4cbb3-7160-5663-9f62-7416ecf098d0" :step/id #uuid "87d4cbb3-7160-5663-9f62-7416ecf098d0" :step/content 17592186064956 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111394 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1fd1460fe24739d1c1bf1a7cad39da6775860b0764eaa4b41fffaae073e3a50.png)\n\nGiven the picture shown above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $E$, $G$ and $H$ are collinear\n\nII. $A$, $B$ and $G$ are **not** collinear\n\nIII. $B$, $F$ and $H$ are collinear" :question/worked-solution "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. There is a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](images/fb/fb82255ac578efc90bb070dab669a19950387cab7d9011de4d5d0d94dd9de505.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](images/2c/2c20ebe076b3e655b7709ccb337c68d4aa8505bfabdb5a8755e5a1410642d612.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](images/98/9859ea0144923d69e37d64130910608f6fc3e7afe09d74af19a5402030e0d3cb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110089 :answer-field/correct 17592186110091 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-73e5dd6e-2c47-5055-afc2-9cc17f2d620e" :question/id #uuid "73e5dd6e-2c47-5055-afc2-9cc17f2d620e" :question/math-academy-id "q-155031" :question/problem "![](images/97/97faa2ca35485f76f012475a9ba2b6af7938cfc86728bddd560cf3da59fff197.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" :question/worked-solution "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/f5/f52592db0fee86e26318d6d2de9d7f685f1fb9f5d416628a04878857f1c54b54.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/76/76f0da764caf0237a2d30d32d593886d8df6c348368fe889f7447be781b6faf1.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/97/9778abd955cdc9b705800eba8f3d0d635af1c6a5fd5724583bdb32a943c6d44f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c338420-c385-5c49-a1ce-a9b479af623d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c338420-c385-5c49-a1ce-a9b479af623d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c338420-c385-5c49-a1ce-a9b479af623d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f3622c66-9de1-5f33-ba19-0af4be6d1d74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2caca10d-0d8d-59c0-ac12-d60468871006" "answer-f3622c66-9de1-5f33-ba19-0af4be6d1d74" "answer-7b3c3526-8bc0-510b-9c22-722374cff8f4" "answer-23cd9f25-8435-55d5-b158-0660cc1531d5" "answer-d4bd80d9-e2bb-50d2-8715-080b48547162"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2caca10d-0d8d-59c0-ac12-d60468871006" :answer/id #uuid "2caca10d-0d8d-59c0-ac12-d60468871006" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3622c66-9de1-5f33-ba19-0af4be6d1d74" :answer/id #uuid "f3622c66-9de1-5f33-ba19-0af4be6d1d74" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b3c3526-8bc0-510b-9c22-722374cff8f4" :answer/id #uuid "7b3c3526-8bc0-510b-9c22-722374cff8f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23cd9f25-8435-55d5-b158-0660cc1531d5" :answer/id #uuid "23cd9f25-8435-55d5-b158-0660cc1531d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4bd80d9-e2bb-50d2-8715-080b48547162" :answer/id #uuid "d4bd80d9-e2bb-50d2-8715-080b48547162" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f44be398-7e63-5657-97ac-ad7b78dba187" :question/id #uuid "f44be398-7e63-5657-97ac-ad7b78dba187" :question/math-academy-id "q-155051" :question/problem "![](images/01/01aba7550fb134b1f3a93276dc8f3b91794844ea21254c2c75a0702a443a60e3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" :question/worked-solution "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/63/6310ff39996be529bc44e5058ef9710400f0c8089196a57e7f4eb541703e53ae.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/63/6334d2bc1fda6390b6ec542afb8cc37d4f76322b98d41ca6bbb3af3c8d735c8f.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/89/8939cff2d7cc8ca9a08c7be63517ffb900e49b769edc5c2454119f4da25904cc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff504920-6a29-5379-af64-ee43cc431a4a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff504920-6a29-5379-af64-ee43cc431a4a" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff504920-6a29-5379-af64-ee43cc431a4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec66f155-3fe5-5781-8a8a-8f6d97fb5483" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4160685f-7ffd-5ade-9845-5db543cb5598" "answer-ec66f155-3fe5-5781-8a8a-8f6d97fb5483" "answer-1fe09bc4-31c7-568f-be87-02c28b0907ab" "answer-88414189-b1f0-5218-8c18-851c2eff974f" "answer-c0e424dc-7e89-5bd6-b6e8-84d2d9dea85f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4160685f-7ffd-5ade-9845-5db543cb5598" :answer/id #uuid "4160685f-7ffd-5ade-9845-5db543cb5598" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec66f155-3fe5-5781-8a8a-8f6d97fb5483" :answer/id #uuid "ec66f155-3fe5-5781-8a8a-8f6d97fb5483" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fe09bc4-31c7-568f-be87-02c28b0907ab" :answer/id #uuid "1fe09bc4-31c7-568f-be87-02c28b0907ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88414189-b1f0-5218-8c18-851c2eff974f" :answer/id #uuid "88414189-b1f0-5218-8c18-851c2eff974f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0e424dc-7e89-5bd6-b6e8-84d2d9dea85f" :answer/id #uuid "c0e424dc-7e89-5bd6-b6e8-84d2d9dea85f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-1e277d26-3cd4-5f96-8fca-4798fc8680e1" :activity/id #uuid "1e277d26-3cd4-5f96-8fca-4798fc8680e1" :activity/title "Collinear Points" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049892 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-31152b20-4269-5712-98a1-7b73a2698d69" "step-765a5c07-dcf7-5bfa-9917-01071a277a0b" "step-2a5c9c16-0e21-58e2-96cf-f2e548e46cfb"] :activity/first-step "step-31152b20-4269-5712-98a1-7b73a2698d69" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-31152b20-4269-5712-98a1-7b73a2698d69" :step/id #uuid "31152b20-4269-5712-98a1-7b73a2698d69" :step/content 17592186111394 :step/next "step-765a5c07-dcf7-5bfa-9917-01071a277a0b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-765a5c07-dcf7-5bfa-9917-01071a277a0b" :step/id #uuid "765a5c07-dcf7-5bfa-9917-01071a277a0b" :step/content "question-73e5dd6e-2c47-5055-afc2-9cc17f2d620e" :step/next "step-2a5c9c16-0e21-58e2-96cf-f2e548e46cfb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2a5c9c16-0e21-58e2-96cf-f2e548e46cfb" :step/id #uuid "2a5c9c16-0e21-58e2-96cf-f2e548e46cfb" :step/content "question-f44be398-7e63-5657-97ac-ad7b78dba187" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204098 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3cd8cc0b-abb0-561c-b8fb-73430e8105db" "question-cfa20750-c86e-5358-ae72-96466e6a1578" "question-5b9f6727-3d7f-553c-90bd-4d6b87d491ad" "question-43f51336-4d87-5e3a-8c17-d2fcf2b0a3a3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203868 :question/problem "Consider the functions $\\mathbf y: \\mathbb R^3\\to \\mathbb R^2$ and $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^2\\to \\mathbb R^2,$ defined by\n![](images/cd/cd2fdbccada4cc5278411db56d86dc72e44aa183fbe9a0c564c4eee587072fef.svg)\n\nDetermine the dimensions of the matrix representing the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{f}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{y}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{y}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}= \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 3$ matrix is a $2\\times 3$ matrix." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204099 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dac59390-e4c1-5a03-ac45-73be8ac0d42f" "question-78eabbdb-ae14-5218-80c9-de54367d95a9" "question-5bd6ce37-dfc0-53de-b48f-f0c1cbf86c1e" "question-316244d1-8305-5968-b730-d6310a788240" "question-fdb98924-99f5-55a4-8e7e-a090dae96539"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203869 :question/problem "Consider the functions $\\mathbf y: \\mathbb R^3\\to \\mathbb R^2$ and $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^2\\to \\mathbb R^2,$ defined by\n![](images/82/823c7500670ec7deeb16b8f656904010f2a579d40f8967ee5f9948f93a2d0c3d.svg)\nWhat is \n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))$ evaluated at \n![](images/31/311debaad69f328c5e9acc9ec0b218eded626be5a9e93f5b07837dc4c296fdaa.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{f}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{y}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ![](images/46/46e607310554414649de798f3b01d1965364d6d652e2ab308d4e5c2647d93ca2.svg) we get\n![](images/95/950fed4e51766a66f71026c05093c38764bc1f0bc9c4c5136015a4187c2c1154.svg)\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{y}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -{x}_{3} & -{x}_{2} \\\\ 1 & {x}_{3} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ![](images/54/547759836320b9850df4a70d8ba19a05741412cc0935dcc43caf8224da07fdce.svg) we get\n![](images/94/94bf8ffbcce681efde92719b220545ac89cdee324cd4dedf391b62e916a3ee8e.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204100 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a62becc2-1daf-5292-96cb-be1f422032a1" "question-622e64f1-e6e7-5b08-85d4-13a13c5f884f" "question-19d96d91-ae11-5a12-a4f6-96b41a2e839b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203873 :question/problem "Consider the functions $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{y},$ where $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^3, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ and the matrices $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ are given by\n![](images/93/93f1a584378171c20649f8c1505162f580fad7074bbb73a571b2eff1ef53b57e.svg)\nWhat is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_0 = [1, \\, 0, \\, -2]^T?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{B}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} = \\mathbf{B}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{A}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = \\mathbf{A}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 20 & -10 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204101 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b20e05f8-4c24-503b-a4d3-441eb6663ecb" "question-c217a4e9-7d66-55f8-9bd7-c609e2aa5841" "question-84a8217d-9b8f-5f74-ba0c-1dc1b7379e21" "question-92b16d62-78ea-5a5a-bcd9-f319e1b69729" "question-1c1ab071-19e5-52c5-b033-ca671fa83041"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203870 :question/problem "For $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y, \\mathbf z\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)))$ evaluated at ![](images/76/7625625edbb65c637775812baec802a512b580b45e94c896764377cd23a0f946.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf z} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf z}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{z} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{C}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf z} = \\mathbf{C}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{z}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n![](images/aa/aa560155df00efd7959768d56e38ef7fea999b06d2bfe5515b1af01f2184aaf4.svg)\nEvaluating at ![](images/9e/9ee03503efd8d3da6a4aa7e012eef1d0e390d014aa6ceae1b0f18acd82b5c08f.svg) we get\n![](images/73/73522ffceb4c0e0f4d3cad330414660ad822609c67a0a0024cdab2cb62c13778.svg)\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{A}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = \\mathbf{A}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3cd8cc0b-abb0-561c-b8fb-73430e8105db" :question/id #uuid "3cd8cc0b-abb0-561c-b8fb-73430e8105db" :question/math-academy-id "q-270266" :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}^{3}-5{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{5} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 3$ matrix is a $2\\times 3$ matrix." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2fdc84c-26a3-52ee-83fa-56334143f205" "answer-field-2dab6e0f-02b6-5411-b63f-a1bfaa3fd0fd" "answer-field-96b42a37-2c60-551c-8e86-744ef7115fed" "answer-field-7812b0c8-a1a4-5e93-9f3d-10a8a8685414" "answer-field-db184784-f6d4-5c28-8a15-c15b05c79c8f" "answer-field-9c7d5f96-b21f-5a6d-9152-c11e8ec482aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2fdc84c-26a3-52ee-83fa-56334143f205" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2fdc84c-26a3-52ee-83fa-56334143f205" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4cbfad5d-2e5e-5ad2-9a54-aab932b7eb19" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4cbfad5d-2e